g bends are editor-selected, not a measured continuous centerline or particle path.\",\r\n      \"route_extent_kind\": \"155 W to 110 W northern Tsuchiya reach\",\r\n      \"approximate_reference_path_km\": 5000,\r\n      \"scenario_range_km\": [\r\n        4900,\r\n        5100\r\n      ],\r\n      \"scenario_count\": 81,\r\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john1806/john1806.shtml\",\r\n      \"source_locator\": \"Sections 2-3 and Table 1: north SSCC peaks at 155 W 3.5 N/220 m and 110 W 4.5 N/130 m. Transport integration latitude limits and 700-dbar reference are separately defined; they are not route width or a uniform axis layer.\",\r\n      \"supporting_sources\": [\r\n        {\r\n          \"url\": \"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john2337/zonal_velocity.shtml\",\r\n          \"citation\": \"Johnson et al. (2002), Direct measurements of upper ocean currents and water properties across the tropical Pacific during the 1990s.\",\r\n          \"locator\": \"Section 3.1: northern subsurface countercurrent/Tsuchiya name; 140 W around 4 N/200 m; eastward shoaling and mean/synoptic differences.\",\r\n          \"role\": \"Later naming and layer context; not an additional dated or uniform-depth route.\"\r\n        }\r\n      ],\r\n      \"candidate_file\": \"research/pacific-nscc-reach-reference-path-candidate.json\",\r\n      \"candidate_sha256\": \"0afb68c180bb6a8528173974c6a88641f1f0a22ddc1db5683ee1f95c68760c33\",\r\n      \"generator_sha256\": \"1362693ec7075e014d065691923248db455a5c3cd78c9a94880b3b472594d240\",\r\n      \"figure\": \"figures/pacific-nscc-reach-reference-path-candidate.svg\",\r\n      \"nominal_atlas_state_crossings\": [\r\n        \"PEQD\",\r\n        \"PNEC\"\r\n      ],\r\n      \"recommended_nasa_tile_id\": \"level2_A_3\",\r\n      \"remaining_gates\": [\r\n        \"Review interpolation against compatible northern Tsuchiya Jet velocity fields; selected section gates are not origins or terminations.\",\r\n        \"Resolve western formation and eastward turning/merging separately from this studied reach.\",\r\n        \"Extract month/depth-resolved axes before annual length or width ranges.\",\r\n        \"Keep NEUC at 130 E and the southern Tsuchiya branches as distinct identities; no transported-volume-to-length or integration-band-to-width conversion.\"\r\n      ],\r\n      \"parent_current_id\": null,\r\n      \"parent_scope_note\": null,\r\n      \"ordering_sensitivity\": null\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"id\": \"pacific-sscc-mean-reach-reference-path-candidate\",\r\n      \"current_id\": \"pacific-south-subsurface-countercurrent\",\r\n      \"name\": \"Pacific South Subsurface Countercurrent\",\r\n      \"status\": \"editorial_reference_path_candidate_not_scientifically_reviewed\",\r\n      \"comparison_group\": \"osw_studied_reach_routes\",\r\n      \"scope\": \"Declared historical southern mean-core studied reach from 155 W 3.5 S to 110 W 5.5 S. These gates truncate the larger southern subsurface flow. Western formation, secondary-jet geometry and eastern continuation are excluded. This is one study-defined connection, not an aggregate centerline or sum of primary and secondary branches.\",\r\n      \"route_extent_kind\": \"155 W to 110 W historical southern mean-core reach\",\r\n      \"approximate_reference_path_km\": 5000,\r\n      \"scenario_range_km\": [\r\n        4900,\r\n        5100\r\n      ],\r\n      \"scenario_count\": 81,\r\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.pmel.noaa.gov/pubs/outstand/john1806/john1806.shtml\",\r\n      \"source_locator\": \"Sections 2-3 and Table 1: south SSCC peaks at 155 W 3.5 S/250 m and 110 W 5.5 S/160 m. Latitude integration bands are not widths. Rowe et al. (2000), introduction and section 3e/Fig. 10 caption, distinguish primary and secondary southern jets.\",\r\n      \"supporting_sources\": [\r\n        {\r\n          \"url\": \"https://floats.pmel.noaa.gov/sites/default/files/atoms/files/gcj_1y.pdf\",\r\n          \"citation\": \"Rowe, Firing and Johnson (2000), Pacific Equatorial Subsurface Countercurrent Velocity, Transport, and Potential Vorticity, JPO 30, 1172-1187.\",\r\n          \"locator\": \"Introduction pp. 1172-1174; data methods pp. 1174-1176; section 3e/Fig. 10 caption p. 1183.\",\r\n          \"role\": \"Primary/secondary naming and unresolved splitting/independent origin; front scale is not whole-current width. No route digitized from figures.\",\r\n          \"license_note\": \"PDF states AMS copyright; no open license inferred; figures and data not packaged.\"\r\n        }\r\n      ],\r\n      \"candidate_file\": \"research/pacific-sscc-mean-reach-reference-path-candidate.json\",\r\n      \"candidate_sha256\": \"bb9b13844a2eb7b52c2a25689a900c3f161841acf13a90240b4a7767df57eed1\",\r\n      \"generator_sha256\": \"1362693ec7075e014d065691923248db455a5c3cd78c9a94880b3b472594d240\",\r\n      \"figure\": \"figures/pacific-sscc-mean-reach-reference-path-candidate.svg\",\r\n      \"nominal_atlas_state_crossings\": [\r\n        \"PEQD\",\r\n        \"PNEC\"\r\n      ],\r\n      \"recommended_nasa_tile_id\": \"level2_A_4\",\r\n      \"remaining_gates\": [\r\n        \"Review correspondence of historical mean-core sections to later primary/secondary labels; no branch aggregation inferred.\",\r\n        \"Review interpolation with compatible southern-jet velocity fields and independently resolve secondary-jet geometry.\",\r\n        \"Obtain month/depth axes before seasonal dimensions; source compilation years are not route observation dates.\",\r\n        \"Resolve western formation and eastern continuation; transport bands and PV-front spans must remain separate from current width.\"\r\n      ],\r\n      \"parent_current_id\": null,\r\n      \"parent_scope_note\": null,\r\n      \"ordering_sensitivity\": null\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"id\": \"north-pacific-reference-path-candidate\",\r\n      \"current_id\": \"north-pacific\",\r\n      \"name\": \"North Pacific Current\",\r\n      \"status\": \"editorial_reference_path_candidate_not_scientifically_reviewed\",\r\n      \"comparison_group\": \"osw_approximate_reference_routes\",\r\n      \"scope\": \"NOAA glossary regional extent from about 160 degrees E to an OSW gate at 145 degrees W, somewhat beyond the source's approximate 150 degrees W region. Alternative ends at 150 degrees W. This convention excludes downstream Alaska/California branches and the Kuroshio Extension. It is not the entire Japan-to-North-America circulation or a universal naming extent; AMS describes a different western origin near 170 degrees W.\",\r\n      \"route_extent_kind\": \"NOAA North Pacific regional extent\",\r\n      \"approximate_reference_path_km\": 4700,\r\n      \"scenario_range_km\": [\r\n        3900,\r\n        5000\r\n      ],\r\n      \"scenario_count\": 162,\r\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/glossary.html\",\r\n      \"source_locator\": \"North Pacific Current entry: eastward flow from approximately 160 degrees E to beyond approximately 150 degrees W, continuing Kuroshio Extension and branching south. Kuroshio Extension entry separates the preceding component.\",\r\n      \"supporting_sources\": [\r\n        {\r\n          \"url\": \"https://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/north-pacific-current/\",\r\n          \"citation\": \"American Meteorological Society, Glossary of Meteorology, North Pacific Current entry.\",\r\n          \"locator\": \"Opening definition: northern subtropical-gyre limb, origin near 170 degrees W and mixing with Aleutian flow.\",\r\n          \"role\": \"Contrasting naming convention; not used to silently extend or redefine this NOAA-convention candidate.\"\r\n        }\r\n      ],\r\n      \"candidate_file\": \"research/north-pacific-reference-path-candidate.json\",\r\n      \"candidate_sha256\": \"ee25ff511216b68828cc270c19499fd16dbeed200f1c2f387072a07813a05d87\",\r\n      \"generator_sha256\": \"1362693ec7075e014d065691923248db455a5c3c